^^More partitive^^

Below you will find a lot more examples using the partitive case.

Short bullet list:
- use with uncountable nouns
- endings mostly -a/-ta (-ä/-tä) *kylmä* --> *kylmää*, *vesi* --> *vettä*
- a word ending in -nen --> sta/stä: *ihminen --> ihmistä*
- use only on the object, not the subject of the sentence
- *rakastaa*, *vihata*, *odottaa* always require partitive: *Odotan ihmistä.* (I am waiting for the person.)
- partitive with numbers except for number 1: *yksi kissa*, *kaksi kissaa*
- if one noun gets partitive, all adjectives/pronouns that describe it also get partitive: *kaksi isoa nopeaa kaunista kissaa* (two big quick beautiful cats)
- 'sinä' in partitive is *sinua* and 'hän' is *häntä*: *Rakastan sinua.* (I love you.)


^Additional information^
________________________________________________________________________________

Example 1:
luen kirjan
luen kirjaa (partitive)

Both the above examples mean 'I read a book', but while the first one means that you will read a book (completely, to the end), the second one means that you are reading an unspecified amount of the book, you are reading in a book continuously, but not necessarily the whole book. 

Additionally, *luen kirjan* would be understood as 'I *will* read the book', because it denotes a finished action (reading the whole book) = future tense. The partitive is thus used for things you are doing right now in this second (something you can't possibly have finished yet). Sounds simple so far?

________________________________________________________________________________

Example 2:
tapaan miehen
tapaan miestä (partitive)

Here, the first sentence means 'I (will) meet a man' (completed) while the second one means, you guessed it, 'I meet a man continuously', or more correctly, 'I am seeing a man' (romantically).

_________________________________________________________________________________

Example 3:
syön banaanin (I will eat a banana)
syön banaania (I am eating banana, could be a whole, could be half, could be 34 bananas. We will not know until I am done.)

________________________________________________________________________________

Example 4:
rakastan sinua (partitive)

The above example means 'I love you'. With the word *rakastaa* (to love) you always need to use partitive, e.g. you don't love someone wholly, just an unspecified amount of that person, continuously. This verb cannot be used with 'sinut', 'kirjan' etc. If you want to say 'I love the dog' you would say *rakastan koiraa*, not *koiran*. You can think of it the other way around: you either love someone or you don't, there is no way to express it in a finished sense (e.g. 'I will love you tomorrow').

Other verbs that always require partitive are for example *odottaa* (to wait), *auttaa* (to help) and *vihata* (to hate).

________________________________________________________________________________

Example 5:
ammun kanin (I (will) shoot a rabbit (and it will be dead afterward, I will not stop before it is dead))
ammun kania (I am shooting *toward* a rabbit. I don't specify whether it dies, if it even hits the rabbit or how many bullets I shoot.)

You could add *ammun kania kaksi kertaa* (I shoot a rabbit two times), thus meaning that you are to shoot a rabbit two times and not more (thus making it into future tense, but the outcome is still unclear). Note that if you use this with the first example, e.g. *ammun kanin kaksi kertaa*, it would mean that you are to shoot the same rabbit dead two times and it somehow geting revived in between the shots.

________________________________________________________________________________

Example 6:
yksi kirja
kaksi kirjaa
kolme kirjaa
viisi miestä
kymmenen suomalaista

This one you already know; after a number other than one, kirja get's another 'a', and this is because a number other than 1 demands partitive case (disregarding that it usually is used for unspecified amounts).

________________________________________________________________________________

Example 7:
minulla ei ole kirjaa (I don't have a/the book)

This is a fun example, because while negation always requires partitive, it can also be (somewhat poetically) combined with the same word in genitive (possession): minulla ei ole *kirjan kirjaa* (I don't even have the book of a book (meaning I have literally no books whatsoever, usually in a negative sense)).

________________________________________________________________________________

Finally, all words that describe a noun that is in partitive also have to take partitive themselves:

Example 8:
Näen ison punaisen talon. (I see the big red house)
Rakastan iso*a* punais*ta* talo*a*. (I love the big red house)

________________________________________________________________________________

Example 9:
banaani on herkullinen (the banana is delicious)
banaani on herkullista (banana is delicious)

In this example, the word *banaani* stays the same and it is the *adjective* that takes the partitive, but why? 

Saying *banaania on herkullista* or *banaania on herkullinen* is wrong, because partitive can only be used on the object of the sentence, e.g. the thing that receives the doing. In this case the banana is 'the doer', 'is' is what's being done, and 'delicious' is the target. 'Delicious' is the object (the thing being done to) and thus takes on the partitive, if you want to say that banana in general is delicious. *banaani on herkullista ruokaa* (banana is delicious food) is also correct, because the food is uncountable (of course) and the banana is the subject of the sentence (the doer).

________________________________________________________________________________

Example 10:
Lainaan tämän kirjan
Lainaan tätä kirjaa

Here, both examples mean ‘I borrow this book’, but the first one is only used when borrowing a book from a library, while the second one is used in all other contexts. This is regardless of the fact that in both scenarios you borrow ‘an equal amount’ of the book.

________________________________________________________________________________

Example 11:
Banaani on hyvää/pahaa
Ihminen on hyvä/paha

You might remember this from Unit 0, where it was explained briefly that if you talk about food being good or bad, you have to use partitive, and if you talk about righteousness or evil, you need to use the normal word. An exception to this if you say, for example, *teet minulle pahaa* (you are doing evil to me, or more correctly, you are hurting me). This should be obvious from context, but when you yourself are speaking or writing, you need to remember this distinction.


[some of the above examples have been borrowed and modified from the wikipedia page on Finnish partitive case, and a few others are just coincidentally similar to those because partitive case is best explained by simple sentences a learner would already know]